---
// Public FAQ. Question data feeds both the rendered page and the FAQPage structured data.
import "../styles/machina.css";

const faqs = [
  {
    tag: "positioning",
    q: "How is Bellamente different from GBrain, Mem0, Zep, and other agent-memory tools?",
    a: "They share plumbing — an embedded Postgres + pgvector store is a commodity now — but they solve different problems. GBrain is an authored markdown \"second brain\": you write a git repo of notes and it builds a typed knowledge graph. Hosted tools like Mem0 and Zep keep your memory on their servers. Bellamente is runtime agent memory that lives entirely on your machine: the agent writes and recalls at runtime, every recall leaves a durable trace you can read, corrections are versioned and forgetting is reversible, and an OpenAI-compatible proxy can ground any chat. It deliberately has no knowledge graph and sends zero telemetry. Same engine room, different ship.",
  },
  {
    tag: "privacy",
    q: "Where does my data live? Is any of it sent anywhere?",
    a: "Everything stays on your machine — one embedded database file in your app directory. The server binds to 127.0.0.1 by default and sends no telemetry. There is no cloud account and nothing phones home; even the bug reporter just prints a prefilled GitHub link for you to submit yourself.",
  },
  {
    tag: "clients",
    q: "Which agents and clients work with it?",
    a: "It is MCP-native — bella mcp plugs into Claude Code, Cursor, Codex, and Cline. Any OpenAI-compatible client works through the proxy by changing one base URL. And any agent can call the plain HTTP API directly.",
  },
  {
    tag: "setup",
    q: "Do I need Docker, a database server, or a model server?",
    a: "No. It is one binary with embedded Postgres (PGlite + pgvector) and local embedding models built in. Run npm install -g bellamente (or pipx install bellamente), then bella serve. First boot creates the database and downloads the model automatically.",
  },
  {
    tag: "platforms",
    q: "What platforms are supported?",
    a: "Windows x64 and Linux x64 today. macOS binaries are not published yet — it is on the roadmap. You can also run it on any platform from source with Bun.",
  },
  {
    tag: "trust",
    q: "How do I trust what it remembered — or catch a bad memory?",
    a: "That is the whole point. Every recall is logged with the query, the candidates, and their scores; every correction is a new version and the old one stays; forgetting is reversible and audited. You can inspect all of it in a local dashboard or query it with plain SQL.",
  },
  {
    tag: "security",
    q: "Can it leak my API keys or secrets into memory?",
    a: "It is built to avoid that. The agent is instructed not to store credentials, and a deterministic gate redacts API keys, tokens, and private keys from memory content and metadata before storage. We are honest about the limit: it catches real credential formats, not every conceivable secret.",
  },
  {
    tag: "license",
    q: "Is it free and open source?",
    a: "Yes — MIT licensed and free. The source is on GitHub and contributions are welcome.",
  },
  {
    tag: "status",
    q: "Is it production-ready?",
    a: "It is version 0.1.0 — an early but real, tested MVP. It is usable today; the public roadmap is honest about what is still ahead (macOS builds, provider-specific proxy shapes, retrieval evals, and more).",
  },
];
---

